  {
    "episode_id": 50736,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/5/0/50736.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 24,
    "turn_text": "Oh, absolutely. I mean, in Maine, we have more coastline than virtually any other state. It's much longer coast than people think because of our islands and our sort of craggy coast. And last year, we were hit by two severe storms in January, 300-year storms, as we call them. That's something that should ever happen in your lifetime, really, and to happen twice. In the same month, it did a devastating amount of damage to our working waterfront. And by that, we mean the fishing wharfs that are lobster fishermen use that have been in their family for years or the communities that have a port where a ferry lands or where the boaters come in in the summer. So enormous amount of damage. And really, people seeing unprecedented kind of problems. Well, that means you got to prepare for the future, you got to fix what already happened. And so while people may be climate change skeptics or unsure of, you know, what they think about government role, boy, when they're waffles down the first person they call is us and say, you know, I need the money to fix it. I need a solution. What are we going to do in the future to make sure this doesn't happen again? When we talk about climate resilience money, it may sound like some big words, but it's really helping small towns and I live in one. That's to go through a planning process of what can we live with? What can we change? Do we have to move houses back from the water? breakwater. What do we have to do that assistance? And then also the money. This costs millions and millions of dollars to repair these things. And we are proud of our long history on the coast. We are a tourism and fishing economy. And we're like a lot of other states, but we're particularly good example of how hard you can be hit. Our whole delegation, which includes Republicans, Democrats, and independents, is going to work at getting federal funding because we just don't have enough money in our small towns or even in our state to do all this work to bring that back to say, you know, how do we help? How do we fight against this in the future? Because I don't think these weather problems are going away. I don't think we've seen the end of it. And, you know, everybody wants their assistance from government when they see the problem in their backyard.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_01",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
