    "turn_type_label": "Substantive"
  },
  {
    "episode_id": 50736,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/5/0/50736.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 39,
    "turn_text": "Well, yeah, I mean, I think we're an excellent example for the nation. And sometimes because we're smaller, we're not anywhere near as partisan or divisive as many states around the country. We're much more sort of purple, but it's not even purple. It's more like we're a commonsense state. People are much more drawn to the practical solution to things than the ideological solution to things. We've long had congressional seats and gubernatorial seats and legislative seats that go back and forth between Republicans and Democrats and independents. And I don't think any of us think that we're here to speak for a party, we're here to speak for the people that we serve. So I think there's a lot to be learned from what we do. Maine is a state that has embraced climate change because we see the impacts of it so deeply in sea level rise. in our ocean waters warming and the change in species that our fishermen are able to catch. We're on the front lines of many of these things and we've tackled them head on rather than trying to stick our head in the sand and say it doesn't exist or let's pretend we don't believe in science.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_01",
