  },
  {
    "episode_id": 50736,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/5/0/50736.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 62,
    "turn_text": "Well, you know, it's not one of those things that people think, you know, oh, gosh, I want Congress to take this on. But once you start talking to people about it, and certainly if you start talking to young people about it, they're well aware of what it is. So we started a slow fashion caucus. This was kind of an easy thing to do for Maine because, you know, we're more of thrift shop kind of shoppers. We're more of, hey, I still got my grandfather's plaid shirt that he'd handed down to my father. You know, we're not like big dressing up people.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_01",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
      {
        "text": "Young people are aware of the issue when discussed.",
        "confidence": 0.95
      },
      {
        "text": "A slow fashion caucus was established by the speaker.",
        "confidence": 0.94
      },
      {
        "text": "Congress is not commonly seen as a priority for addressing this issue.",
        "confidence": 0.92
      },
      {
        "text": "Maine residents tend to prefer secondhand shopping over new clothing.",
        "confidence": 0.9
      },
      {
        "text": "Some Mainers value family heirlooms, such as inherited clothing items.",
        "confidence": 0.88
      }
    ],
    "assumptions": [
      {
        "text": "Engaging younger generations increases awareness and support for the cause.",
        "confidence": 0.93
      },
      {
        "text": "People generally do not volunteer interest in discussing this topic unless prompted.",
        "confidence": 0.91
      },
      {
        "text": "Cultural practices around clothing reuse reflect deeper values related to sustainability.",
        "confidence": 0.89
      },
      {
        "text": "The speaker’s personal experience with family heirloom clothing reflects broader regional norms.",
        "confidence": 0.87
      },
      {
        "text": "Creating a formal group (caucus) signals legitimacy and organizational commitment.",
        "confidence": 0.86
      }
    ],
    "turn_type_label": "Substantive"
  },
  {
    "episode_id": 50736,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/5/0/50736.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 64,
    "turn_text": "It is a fast fashion and throwaway clothing is now the fastest growing source of waste material in a landfill. So it's costing us money. It's turning into methane gas as it rots away in your landfill. People have started to turn to these fashion companies like Sheen and Timu who get under our tariff regulations and are able to ship to you after you saw something on the internet, you get it home, you find out it's poorly made, very likely doesn't meet with any environmental standards, could have been made by children and 70% of our clothing today is actually made out of a fossil fuel product. As a longtime farmer, I used to be a sheep farmer. I want to see us using more natural materials supporting American farmers, domestic textiles, and getting these toxic out of our waste stream, which either end up in a landfill, could end up in your groundwater, could end up as plastic in the ocean. So we support a whole variety of things to eliminate this. It's also part of my subcommittee to look at how the EPA treats recycling and clothing and fashion has really been left out of understanding our waste stream. Much of it ends up in foreign countries. Or as I said, it costs your municipality because it's a big part of your own landfill.",
